<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mesha Stele</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 840 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> · Also known as Moabite Stone</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesha">Mesha</a> Stele, or Moabite Stone, is a basalt slab inscribed around 840 BCE for King Mesha of Moab, found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhiban">Dhiban</a> in Jordan in 1868 and now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>. Its text, in the Moabite language and alphabet, celebrates Mesha's revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omri">Omri</a>, and his building works. It is the longest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> inscription from the region and one of the earliest references outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> to Israel and its god.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mesha-stele.jpg" alt="Mesha Stele" width="320" height="494" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P1120870_Louvre_st%C3%A8le_de_M%C3%A9sha_AO5066_rwk.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The stele is a smoothed block of basalt about a metre tall, with a surviving inscription of 34 lines. Frederick Augustus Klein, an Anglican missionary, found it intact in August 1868 among the ruins of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhiban">Dibon</a>, now Dhiban in Jordan. News of the find set off a race among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to acquire it. Charles Simon Clermont-Ganneau, at the French consulate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, had a papier-mâché impression, or squeeze, taken of the full stone.</p><p>In November 1869 the Bani Hamida broke the stele after the Ottoman government demanded that it be given to the German consulate. Fragments carrying 613 of about a thousand letters were later recovered, and Clermont-Ganneau reconstructed the rest from the squeeze. The stele has been in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> since 1873, and Jordan has asked for its return since 2014.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesha">Mesha</a>, king of Moab and a Dibonite, records that he built a sanctuary for the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemosh">Chemosh</a> at Qarho, the citadel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhiban">Dibon</a>, in thanks for deliverance from his enemies. He says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omri">Omri</a>, king of Israel, oppressed Moab for many days because Chemosh was angry with his land, and that Omri's son continued this. Mesha describes taking Ataroth from the men of Gad and killing its warriors, and taking Nebo. He lists his building works, including walls, gates, a palace, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> for water and a road across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnon">Arnon</a>, and ends with the capture of Horonaim, where the text breaks off.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The account parallels, with differences, an episode in 2 Kings 3 in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoram-of-israel">Jehoram of Israel</a> campaigned against his rebellious vassal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesha">Mesha</a>. This is the basis of the usual dating of about 840 BCE, though André Lemaire cautions that the identification is not certain and the stele may be as late as 810 BCE. In 1994 Lemaire read a phrase in line 31 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-david">House of David</a>, a reading that others dispute; Matthieu Richelle holds it remains uncertain.</p><p>Some scholars questioned the stele's authenticity after its discovery, but the great majority of biblical archaeologists regard it as genuine, and the Khirbat Ataruz inscribed altar, found in 2010, gave further support. Thomas L. Thompson regards the text as allegory, a view John Emerton and Lemaire have criticised. The stele is the major evidence for the Moabite language.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mesha Stele” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Mesha Stele</b> records that Omri and his son held Moab in subjection until King Mesha rebelled.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The first millennium BCE left inscriptions: the <b>Mesha Stele</b> of Moab (c. 850 BCE) and the Deir Alla inscription (c. 700 BCE); Aramean texts such as the Tell Fekherya bilingual, the Stele of Zakkur (c. 775 BCE) and the Sefire steles; Phoenician ones such as the Yehimilk inscription…</blockquote><a href="/religion/canaanite-religion">Canaanite religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Mesha Stele</b>, found at Dhiban in 1868, tells how Mesha freed Moab from the Kingdom of Israel after Ahab's dynasty had held it, building towns and reservoirs.</blockquote><a href="/state/moab">Moab</a></li></ul></section>
